Component Explanations
1. Web/Mobile Application
Provides the interface for customers to browse events,
select seats, and purchase tickets.
2. Load Balancer
Distributes incoming requests across multiple application
servers to prevent one server from becoming overloaded.
3. API Gateway
Receives requests and directs them to the appropriate services.
It also handles authentication and request limits.
4. Event Service
Manages event information and event searches.
5. Seat Service
Manages seat availability, temporary reservations, and
seat locking.
6. Order Service
Creates and manages customer orders.
7. Payment Service
Processes payments and verifies successful transactions.
8. Redis Cache
Stores frequently requested information such as event details
and seat availability to reduce database workload.
9. Waiting Queue
Controls the number of customers accessing the purchasing
system during popular ticket sales.
10. PostgreSQL Database
Stores users, events, seats, orders, and booking information.
11. Ticket Service
Generates electronic tickets after successful payment.
12. Notification Service
Sends booking confirmations through email or SMS.
How the Architecture Handles Big Sales
During a popular concert sale, 200,000 customers may attempt
to purchase 20,000 seats within ten minutes.
The architecture handles this by:
•	Using a waiting queue to control incoming customers.
•	Using load balancing to distribute requests.
•	Using Redis caching to reduce repeated database queries.
•	Using database transactions to prevent double booking.
•	Using multiple application servers to support increased traffic.
•	Using asynchronous notifications to avoid delaying purchases.
7. System Design Trade-offs
Trade-off 1: Consistency vs Availability
TicketHub prioritizes data consistency during seat purchases.
Although some requests may take longer during heavy traffic,
the system ensures that one seat cannot be sold to multiple
customers.
Trade-off 2: Caching vs Data Accuracy
Redis caching improves speed and reduces database workload.
However, cached seat information may become outdated.
Therefore, event information can be cached, but actual seat
availability must always be verified against the database
before a reservation is confirmed.
Trade-off 3: Fairness vs Speed
A waiting queue improves fairness by controlling access
during popular sales.
However, customers may experience waiting times before
reaching the purchasing system.
8. Conclusion
TicketHub is designed to provide a reliable, scalable, and
secure online ticketing platform.
The combination of load balancing, caching, waiting queues,
database transactions, and relational constraints ensures
that the platform can handle normal traffic and major concert
sales while preventing double booking.
The system prioritizes correctness, speed, fairness, and
customer satisfaction.

